1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,851
The man who took these photos had been dead for thirty-three years when they were developed.

2
00:00:05,901 --> 00:00:09,928
The film lay waiting under the ice, on an island in the Arctic.

3
00:00:09,978 --> 00:00:14,250
This is the story of three men who tried to fly to the North Pole by balloon.

4
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:19,465
The year is 1897.

5
00:00:19,515 --> 00:00:22,351
No one had reached the North Pole yet.

6
00:00:22,401 --> 00:00:28,609
Swedish engineer Salomon August Andrée had a bold plan: to go not by ship, but by air.

7
00:00:28,659 --> 00:00:36,535
Two young men went with him: 27-year-old engineer Knut Frænkel and 24-year-old photographer Nils Strindberg.

8
00:00:36,585 --> 00:00:40,291
The expedition was backed by Alfred Nobel and the King of Sweden.

9
00:00:40,341 --> 00:00:44,808
A huge wooden hangar was built on Danskøya, in Svalbard.

10
00:00:44,858 --> 00:00:48,115
The balloon was called Örnen, the Eagle.

11
00:00:48,165 --> 00:00:50,797
July 11, 1897.

12
00:00:50,847 --> 00:00:52,261
The balloon took off.

13
00:00:52,311 --> 00:00:57,660
But in the very first minutes, two of the three ropes used to steer the balloon broke off.

14
00:00:57,710 --> 00:01:00,377
The Eagle was now at the mercy of the wind.

15
00:01:00,427 --> 00:01:02,619
The balloon flew for sixty-five hours.

16
00:01:02,669 --> 00:01:05,752
Fog and ice weighed the balloon down.

17
00:01:05,802 --> 00:01:09,557
On July 14, the Eagle came down in the middle of the polar ice.

18
00:01:09,607 --> 00:01:15,451
Strindberg photographed the moment: a collapsed balloon and endless white.

19
00:01:15,501 --> 00:01:17,509
There was only one way out: to walk.

20
00:01:17,559 --> 00:01:23,072
The three men began dragging sledges and a boat across the ice.

21
00:01:23,122 --> 00:01:25,693
The sledges weighed more than two hundred kilos.

22
00:01:25,743 --> 00:01:29,139
They had to push the boat between blocks of ice.

23
00:01:29,189 --> 00:01:32,107
When food ran low, they hunted polar bears.

24
00:01:32,157 --> 00:01:34,839
But there was something they did not realize.

25
00:01:34,889 --> 00:01:40,411
As they walked south, the ice floe they were walking on was drifting them back.

26
00:01:40,461 --> 00:01:44,476
They walked for weeks, but made almost no progress.

27
00:01:44,526 --> 00:01:50,383
In early October, about three months later, they sighted land: Kvitøya, White Island.

28
00:01:50,432 --> 00:01:51,917
They set up camp.

29
00:01:51,967 --> 00:01:56,294
A few days later, Andrée's diary fell silent.

30
00:01:56,344 --> 00:01:58,740
All three died within a few weeks.

31
00:01:58,790 --> 00:02:02,226
Even today, no one knows for certain why they died.

32
00:02:02,276 --> 00:02:05,792
For thirty-three years, the world did not know what had happened.

33
00:02:05,842 --> 00:02:12,415
In the summer of 1930, the crew of the Norwegian ship Bratvaag landed on White Island and found the camp.

34
00:02:12,466 --> 00:02:16,996
The bodies, the diaries and Strindberg's rolls of film were there.

35
00:02:17,046 --> 00:02:20,074
The film was carefully developed in Stockholm.

36
00:02:20,124 --> 00:02:23,976
And thirty-three years later, the images slowly appeared.

37
00:02:24,026 --> 00:02:27,934
Most of the photos you are seeing today came from that film.

38
00:02:27,984 --> 00:02:32,386
The three men were laid to rest in Stockholm, in a ceremony attended by the King.

39
00:02:32,436 --> 00:02:34,962
They never reached the North Pole by balloon.

40
00:02:35,012 --> 00:02:40,859
But Nils Strindberg's photographs came out from under the ice and told their story themselves.
